# Meow Bot — environment template.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in the values marked REQUIRED. Everything else has a
# working default: a blank optional value switches that feature off rather than
# half-enabling it. `.env` is excluded from images and from git — only this file
# is committed.
DISCORD_TOKEN=paste_your_bot_token_here
FLASK_SECRET_KEY=generate_a_long_random_string
ADMIN_PASSKEY=choose_a_strong_unique_passkey
DASHBOARD_BASE_URL=https://meow-bot.pages.dev

# Discord sign-in for the dashboard: the way a server's own administrators get
# in without being handed the operator's passkey or a one-time link. With these
# set, /login offers "Sign in with Discord"; the person sees only the servers
# where they hold Manage Server (or Administrator, or own it) AND the bot is
# already in, and the session can act on nothing else.
#
# Both halves come from the Developer Portal -> your app -> OAuth2. The client
# secret is a server-side credential: never put it in a page, a repository or
# Discord. Register this exact redirect URL on that same page, or Discord
# refuses every sign-in with a redirect_uri mismatch — the login page prints the
# URL to register for the host it is actually running on:
#
#   {DASHBOARD_BASE_URL}/login/discord/callback
#
# Leave both blank to switch the method off; the passkey and the one-time link
# keep working either way.
DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=
DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Which roles may use /market. This is a gate, not a permission: a member either
# holds one of these roles or is refused, and administrators are not an exception.
# Comma-separated for more than one role. Leave blank to use the compiled default
# (the role in utils/market.py DEFAULT_ACCESS_ROLE_IDS).
MARKET_ROLE_IDS=

# Optional: post unhandled command errors to a Discord webhook so failures are
# visible instead of being swallowed into stdout. Leave blank to disable.
ERROR_WEBHOOK_URL=

# The anonymous question box: the webhook `/qna ask` posts through, so a question
# reaches the channel with nobody's name on it.
#
# Create it in the channel that should receive the questions — Channel settings
# -> Integrations -> Webhooks -> New Webhook — and paste its URL below. Leave it
# blank and `/qna ask` refuses with a sentence naming this variable rather than
# doing nothing, because a question box with nowhere to post to must not read as
# a broken one.
#
# The URL is a **credential**: anybody holding it can post into that channel as
# the box, which is why it lives in the environment and not behind a command —
# no member, and no dashboard page, can set it. The asker's own name is never
# part of what is posted; it is written beside the question and shown only to
# `OWNER_USER_ID`, who answers it by DM.
QNA_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Optional: a **second** webhook, for the alerts that have to arrive when the
# ordinary path cannot be used — the bot was just added to a server (so it is in
# none of that server's channels yet), the gateway is down (which is why the web
# process is answering at all), or the website is being flooded by an automated
# client. Those are exactly the events a channel inside some server cannot
# receive, so this one is plain HTTP, sent from a thread, and posted **silently**
# (Discord's suppress-notifications flag) — it can be watched without paging
# anybody who is asleep.
#
# It is bounded on purpose: one message per kind of event per 30 seconds, and at
# most 120 per process, because the thing watching a flood must not be the
# amplifier. A join alert names the inviter and how old that account is — a
# hundred invites from accounts minutes old is the shape a raid takes.
#
# Leave blank and nothing is sent; the dashboard's Status page reports that as a
# missing setting rather than as a quiet success. What actually travels is listed
# in full in PRIVACY.md section 5 and TERMS.md section 12: the kind of event, the
# requesting address and user-agent string, the path, the coarse country, the
# counts, and a server's name and id. Nothing else.
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# The server the bot calls home — the one whose #update, #bot-error and #bug
# exist to receive what the bot has to say about itself. Used only as the
# fallback destination for a bug report: a report is posted in the server it was
# filed in whenever that server has a bug channel of its own, and lands here only
# when it does not, so a report is never silently dropped. Leave blank for the
# default server the bot's house channels were created in.
BOT_SERVER_GUILD_ID=

# Where the bot posts its own release notes, and which role those posts ping.
# Blank uses the compiled default, which is a channel and a role in the bot's
# home server — so in any *other* server the announcement is addressed to
# something that does not exist and never arrives. Copy the two ids from the
# server you actually want told (Developer Mode -> right-click -> Copy ID).
UPDATE_CHANNEL_ID=
UPDATE_ROLE_ID=

# The Render deploy hook that /commit fires to redeploy this service: Render ->
# your service -> Settings -> Deploy Hook. Leave it blank and /commit still
# records and announces what it did, but nothing is deployed — which is what it
# reports when used. It is a credential: it deploys on its own.
RENDER_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL=

# How the dashboard's status panel learns whether this deployment has actually
# been *reliable*, rather than only whether it is connected right now. If you
# have pointed an UptimeRobot monitor at this host, that history already exists;
# the **read-only** key (UptimeRobot -> My Settings -> API Settings -> Read-Only
# API Key) is what lets the bot ask what it has seen. It is read-only and still
# worth guarding: it can see every monitor on the account. Nothing is fetched
# while a page is being answered — a background loop refreshes the snapshot and
# the dashboard, /api/uptime and /health all read that.
#
# Blank is a supported state rather than a broken one: the panel says the
# integration is off and names this variable, which is a different sentence from
# the empty list an unconfigured integration would otherwise show — and a very
# different one from "everything is down".
UPTIMEROBOT_API_KEY=

# Optional: the public status page (UptimeRobot -> Status Pages) to link from the
# panel for people who are not signed in. No page, no link.
UPTIMEROBOT_STATUS_PAGE=

# The Discord user id of the bot's owner: the person who may approve the actions
# reserved to them (the admin-limit overrides). Blank leaves nobody the owner, so
# those approvals refuse and say the owner has not been configured.
OWNER_USER_ID=

# The passkey is the door to /owner. Set this to 1 and a Discord sign-in that
# matches OWNER_USER_ID opens the whole fleet by itself — the button on the
# sign-in page becomes a complete sign-in, with no passkey. Off by default,
# because "the owner site never asked for the pass" is exactly that switch.
OWNER_DISCORD_ELEVATION=0

# The shared secret between the owner site's Cloudflare Worker and this app.
#
# The owner host is a proxy of a proxy, so the forwarded address chain ends at
# Cloudflare rather than at the visitor — meaning everybody on that domain was
# counted as one client for sign-in rate limiting, which turned a five-attempt
# limit into a five-attempt *global* lockout any passer-by could trigger. The
# worker now names the visitor in X-Meow-Client-IP and signs it with this
# secret, and the app believes that header only when the signature matches
# (compared in constant time).
#
# One value, two hosts: set it for the Pages build that runs
# scripts/build_owner.py, and here. Leaving both blank keeps the old fallback.
OWNER_PROXY_SECRET=

# Set to 1 only if you put the app behind your own reverse proxy (nginx, etc.)
# so login rate limiting can safely read X-Forwarded-For.
TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=0

# Set to 1 if this host is served over HTTPS and is *not* Render or Railway — the
# two hosts the dashboard recognises without being told. Behind your own TLS
# terminator the session cookie would otherwise be issued without its Secure
# flag, so a plaintext request to the app's own port would carry it.
DASHBOARD_HTTPS=0

# Privileged intents. These stay OFF unless you set them to 1, because requesting
# an intent that isn't enabled in the Developer Portal makes Discord refuse the
# login entirely (the bot would go offline). To turn them on: Developer Portal ->
# your app -> Bot -> Privileged Gateway Intents, enable them there FIRST, then set
# the flags below. Message Content makes AutoMod link filtering and message-length
# XP work; Guild Members enables member join/leave events. Presence is what makes
# "who is online" answerable at all — without it Discord reports every member as
# offline, so the owner console's Live page shows no presence figures at all
# rather than a confident and wrong set of them.
ENABLE_MESSAGE_CONTENT_INTENT=0
ENABLE_MEMBERS_INTENT=0
ENABLE_PRESENCE_INTENT=0

# Supabase Storage (database persistence). The bot uploads the live bot.db here
# roughly every 25 seconds, keeps the newest 5 hourly snapshots for point-in-time
# restore, and restores the newest usable one on boot. The bucket is private:
# every read and write needs this service-role key, and the payload is a raw
# SQLite file, so this key is the whole database's password. Keep it out of the
# dashboard and out of Discord, and rotate it if it ever leaks.
#
# (The older Gist backup and its at-rest encryption key are gone — Supabase
# replaced them, and nothing reads those variables any more.)
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Plans and licences
#
# Where the paid tiers are bought: a Lemon Squeezy **hosted checkout URL** per
# tier (product -> Share -> Copy link). It is public by nature — it is rendered
# into /pricing for anyone to click — and the variant id inside the path is what
# tells the store which product is being bought, so copy the link rather than
# building one by hand.
#
# Setting these does **not** start selling: PLAN_CHECKOUT below does that. Fill
# them in freely while the store is being configured.
LEMONSQUEEZY_CHECKOUT_PRO=
LEMONSQUEEZY_CHECKOUT_ENTERPRISE=

# Optional, and only needed if a product is renamed in the store: these variant
# ids map a licence to a plan without reading the product's name. The ids are
# stable; the name is not. Both are found in the checkout URL (or the store API).
LEMONSQUEEZY_VARIANT_PRO=
LEMONSQUEEZY_VARIANT_ENTERPRISE=

# Whether the plans can be **bought**. Off by default, and held off on purpose
# rather than inferred from the two URLs above: pasting a checkout link while a
# store is being set up is not a decision to start taking money. While this is 0
# no host shows a Buy button — the tiers are still described, and read as where
# the project is heading — even if the links above are filled in. Set to 1 when
# you are ready to sell; the links take effect immediately.
PLAN_CHECKOUT=0

# Whether a licence is **required** for the paid systems. Off by default, and it
# starts off on purpose: switching it on takes Support Tickets, Economy & Casino,
# the Market, Starboard and the Activity Tracker away from every server that has
# not activated a licence — including servers using them today — and holds a free
# server to one server and no scheduled messages. Plans are recorded and shown
# either way; set this to 1 when the tiers should be real.
PLAN_ENFORCEMENT=0

# When a deploy announces itself. A release note goes to every server with a
# bound update channel on each boot that changed CHANGELOG.md, and to a server the
# moment it adds the bot; the home server gets one through its #update channel
# even with nothing bound, which is why a checkout running locally can post a
# note into it. A server could not ask for those to *move*: clearing its binding
# reads as "not chosen yet", so the next boot bound the update channel by name
# again and the notes resumed. These two are that request.
#
# The times of day, in IST, a release note may go out. Three of them, minutes
# apart, because a free host can be asleep at the first. A boot outside the window
# sends nothing and records nothing — the note is still owed, and it is a digest
# of everything since that server's last one, so a delay is a delay and never a
# gap. `always` posts the moment a deploy boots, which is what every deployment
# did before this existed.
UPDATE_WINDOW_IST=03:33,04:00,04:44

# The other end: 1 stops the automatic notes *entirely*, leaving the update
# channel permanently empty, which is what a server that wants no notes at all is
# asking for. `/bot push` still posts one when an operator asks for it, because a
# person asking is not what is being silenced.
SILENT_UPDATES=0
